# Chromeleon architecture (v2)

Chromeleon restyles the **live page**. It does not scrape content into an overlay.
That is what keeps images, buttons, forms, video and site scripts working.

## How it works

1. `src/engine/detect.ts` scores the live DOM (Readability-style) and returns the
   real content root element, plus the title block and lead image if they sit outside it.
2. `src/engine/engine.ts` tags elements with data attributes:
   - `data-chl-root` / `data-chl-keep`: the reading column
   - `data-chl-path`: ancestors of kept elements (their layout gets flattened)
   - `data-chl-hide`: everything else under the path, plus junk inside the article
   - `<html data-chl-on data-chl-mode="reader|whole">`: turns the stylesheet on
3. `src/engine/styles.ts` builds one `<style id="chromeleon-style">`, scoped entirely
   under `html[data-chl-on]`. Removing the attribute restores the original page.
4. A MutationObserver handles SPA navigation, re-rendered articles, late content,
   and new elements: allowed through if the user just clicked, hidden otherwise.

## Rules

- Never clone, move or re-render page content. Tag elements; let CSS do the rest.
- Every injected rule must be scoped under `html[data-chl-on]` so it's inert when off.
- Broad resets go in `:where()`; targeted rules add a type selector to win over them.
- Keep the content script dependency-free (no React): it runs on every page.
- Per-site behaviour comes from `SiteRule` in `src/shared/settings.ts`, not hard-coded hostnames.
- Add a fixture under `test/fixtures/` and checks in `test/e2e.mjs` for every detection change.
